WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.560
- In 2016, a spooky white figure
with wide, unblinking eyes

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.590
was spotted wandering a Green
Bay, Wisconsin parking lot

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.880
at two in the morning.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.550
Residents posted about
their sightings on Facebook

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.410
and made calls to the police.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.290
But the strange figure
continued appearing that night,

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.720
in a local park and under an overpass,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.410
then, it suddenly disappeared
just as mysteriously.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.830
A week later, a similar being
appeared in South Carolina,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.350
beckoning to residents from the woods.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.380
Over the next two months, dozens
of these mysterious figures

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.990
would pop up all across
the U.S. and Canada,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.470
along with sightings in 16 other
countries around the world.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.080
Some would attempt to approach
children to frighten them,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.570
most appeared silently, watching,

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.210
but all of them were dressed as clowns.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.760
Clowns are known for their
humorous antics, balloon animals,

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.490
and juggling to amuse audiences,
whether they're teaching

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.590
us how to tell time on Saturday morning TV

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.240
or selling us french fries.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.310
So when did these silly, funny staples

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.369
of our childhoods become monsters?

00:00:58.369 --> 00:01:00.060
(adventurous music)

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.110
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.810
The history of clowns spans centuries.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.940
We associate them with
bright-colored clothing,

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.780
oddly shaped hats, oversized
shoes, and painted faces.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.030
Our current image of
clowns started taking shape

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:16.830
around the mid-19th century

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.100
when an English comedian and
actor named Joseph Grimaldi

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.440
began performing in a
polka-dotted costume,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.690
pointy, triangular wig, ruffled collar,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.223
and red-and-white face paint.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.100
But since the 1980s,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.860
clowns have taken a decidedly dark turn.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.170
Depictions of clowns have
taken these visual traits

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.780
from kooky to creepy
by playing up features

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.949
like pale skin, big eyes,
and a wide unnerving smile.

00:01:39.949 --> 00:01:41.580
(clown laughs menacingly)

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.800
These creepy clowns appear
like the silly characters

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.140
of our childhoods, just slightly off.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.820
So why the shift?

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.700
Even before killer clowns
were all over our screens,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.170
clown characters have occupied

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.240
an unusual place in the social order.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.360
Some of the most ancient
versions were fairly problematic.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.430
In places like ancient Egypt and Rome,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.910
stigmatized, marginalized
people, many enslaved,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.230
were treated as entertainment

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.360
at parties and events for the wealthy.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.030
Ancient Rome also had mime-type characters

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.700
and ones who performed
unusual body stunts.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.220
Beginning at least as
early as the 13th century,

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.340
Chinese opera featured clown
characters called Chou,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.410
who were often the only ones on stage

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.240
allowed to improvise lines.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.670
And Renaissance-era
court jesters in Europe

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.580
were kept in noble
circles as entertainment

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.470
and were permitted to satirize royalty

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.170
and speak truths without
fearing punishment.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:34.200
Clowns throughout the world
began as trickster characters

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.420
who had a pass to flout social conventions

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.760
and behave in transgressive ways.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.980
By the mid 1800s, clowns began to filter

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.200
from their high-class
entertainment origins

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.730
into popular culture.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.650
Joseph Grimaldi became a celebrity

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.390
in the English theater scene.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.490
Meanwhile, in the U.S.,
the Ringling Brothers

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.620
and Barnum &amp; Bailey set up circus shows

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.010
featuring clowns performing
slapstick comedy routines.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.420
As clowns rose in popularity,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.910
they retained their roguish spirit.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:01.500
It wasn't until literary works

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.300
like Charles Dickens' "Pickwick Papers,"

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.680
Edgar Allan Poe's "Hop-Frog"

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.530
and Ruggero Leoncavallo's
opera "Pagliacci,"

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.530
that the clown persona
took on a much darker tone.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.120
These depictions were some of the first

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.980
to look at the man behind the mask.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.380
Depicting clowns that, underneath
their trickster silliness,

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.005
have sinister or even
murderous intentions.

00:03:19.005 --> 00:03:22.922
(Pagliacci singing in Italian)

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.500
But by the mid-20th century,

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.960
American clowns got a
kid-friendly makeover

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.340
featuring more friendly figures

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.350
like "Howdy Doody's" Clarabell the Clown,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.930
Bozo the Clown, and Ronald McDonald.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.760
This reflected the broad optimism

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.740
and prosperity of the post-war era.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.220
But this wholesome imagining
of the clown didn't last long.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.040
In 1979, serial killer John Wayne Gacy,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.160
who worked as a party clown
as a day job, was arrested.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.800
At the same time, the
Satanic Panic of the 1980s

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.150
was picking up speed.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.520
This moral frenzy based
on conspiracy theories

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.710
that evil cultists were harming children

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.830
made many people anxious
about who they could trust.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.300
Against this cultural backdrop,

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.180
creepy clowns started to
become a pop culture staple,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.550
often using their
kid-friendly cultural status

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.410
to lure children to their deaths.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.150
Their exaggerated facial features

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.250
pushed clowns into the uncanny valley,

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.260
a theorized point where
an object or character

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.180
appears almost like a real person

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.040
without looking fully human.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.570
It's that tiny bit of dissonance

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.510
which fills actual human
viewers with unease.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.610
Film depictions use this creepy dissonance

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.627
to create frightening characters.

00:04:28.627 --> 00:04:31.140
"Poltergeist," a movie in
which a possessed clown toy

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.380
attacks a young boy in his
bedroom, was a box office hit.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.290
And "Batman" starred horror
movie staple Jack Nicholson

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.690
playing Joker as a villainous clown.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.750
Even children's media turned
clowns exclusively scary.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.430
Who else has nightmares

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:45.900
about the evil firefighter clown

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.520
from "Brave Little Toaster?"

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.840
'Cause I hate it.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.770
Perhaps most famously,
Stephen King's 1986 novel "It"

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.100
features Pennywise, an evil
clown that stalks the suburbs

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.230
looking for children to frighten and kill.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:04:59.760
The book became so successful

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.970
that it was adapted as a TV
series by the end of the decade,

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.030
solidifying the clown as not
just a man in face paint,

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:07.740
but a monster that could transform

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.750
into whatever you fear most.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.887
The 1988 cult sci-fi horror comedy

00:05:11.887 --> 00:05:13.770
"Killer Klowns from Outer Space"

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.050
turned this newly-minted
archetype on its head

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:18.390
with a more playful
take on the killer clown

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.910
featuring murderous
aliens disguised as clowns

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.900
who trap their unsuspecting
victims in cotton candy cocoons.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.630
The film's decision to steer
the killer clown archetype

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.640
back towards its whimsical origins

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:33.000
shows how prevalent the creepy
clown phenomenon had become.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.410
The last decade has brought in

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.110
another wave of creepy
clowns in pop culture.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:38.730
Horrifying harlequins cropped up

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.327
in "American Horror Stories,"

00:05:40.327 --> 00:05:42.360
"Supernatural," and "Zombieland."

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.211
- [Columbus] Time to nut up or shut up.

00:05:44.211 --> 00:05:45.907
(clown growls)

00:05:45.907 --> 00:05:47.520
- "It, "The Joker," and "Poltergeist"

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.740
all got darker, grittier reboots,

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.510
but then, the clowns we
love to fear on screen

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:53.370
got a lot less fictional.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.050
In the fall of 2016,

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.420
that mysterious clown
appeared in Wisconsin.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:58.860
It didn't hurt anyone.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.830
It just broke social norms,
as clowns have always done.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.360
By creeping around at odd hours

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.190
and remaining silent when spoken to,

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.590
this clown didn't act quite human.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:08.910
The prankster eventually revealed

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.470
that he was trying to drum up publicity

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.840
for an independent horror
film, and it worked.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:14.760
Photos and stories about
the clown sightings

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.510
went viral on Facebook, inspiring
a wave of copycat clowns

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.360
in more than 20 states
over the next two months.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.370
If you remember the late summer of 2016,

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:24.720
you know that it was a volatile

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.370
and politically divisive time.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.230
It's possible that these tricksters wanted

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:29.370
to let off some of the tension

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.230
that was building in their communities

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:34.590
or to direct anxieties at
a common fictional enemy.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:36.690
But they successfully tapped
into the public's fear

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.730
of these uncanny valley creatures enough

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.250
that some school districts
and municipalities

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.070
warned against, or even
banned, wearing clown costumes

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.010
at Halloween for being symbols of terror.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.750
Police in Utah even received calls

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:50.580
about whether citizens were allowed

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.350
to shoot clowns on sight.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:53.910
And when rumors started circulating

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.760
about a clown on Penn
State's campus that October,

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.220
more than 500 students took to the streets

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:02.640
in a so-called clown riot
to hunt the jester down.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.200
And yet, just like in the '80s,

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.120
clowns were more of a psychological threat

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.080
than a physical one.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.480
With their exaggerated
smiles, it's difficult

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.850
to read what clowns are
really thinking or feeling

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:13.500
underneath all that face paint

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.810
and their reputation
for mischievous behavior

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.450
makes it hard for us to predict
what they might do next.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:21.030
And all of that uncertainty
makes us uneasy.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.710
It's no wonder that killer clowns

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:24.000
tend to emerge in pop culture

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.400
at times of heightened political tension

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.440
or distrust in the social contract.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:29.280
When we're all wondering

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.290
whether we can trust the people around us.

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.600
Clowns may be a symbol of human duality,

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:35.250
one that asks us to consider

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.710
if we all wear a little bit of a costume

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.660
when we present ourselves to the world.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.308
Ruggero Leoncavallo's opera "Pavliacci."

00:07:43.308 --> 00:07:44.730
- [Producer] "Pagliacci."
- "Pagliacci."

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:45.750
There's no V?

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:46.583
- [Producer] Wait there's...

00:07:46.583 --> 00:07:47.416
Oh!
- "Pavliacci?"

00:07:47.416 --> 00:07:49.080
- [Producer] I didn't even pick that up.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.580
- Did I say it right though?

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.063
You're like, "Uh..."

